) 9. Nc4
{Back, a pawn up, and the hole on e6 is worth as much as the pawn. [%tags majority] [%plan Castle, play Re1 and a4, and keep the extra pawn. Black has opened lines at his own king to win it back.] [%line The fianchetto, 3...g6]}
) ( 3... Nge7 {The knight heads for g6 the long way round. [%weight 2]} 4. Bxc6
{Take, and accept a comfortable game rather than a small pull. [%tags doubled-pawns]} (
4. Nc3!?
{The engine's move, and close to a pawn better. It is also a different opening from the rest of this course.}
) 4... dxc6 {Towards the centre. [%weight 7]} 5. Nc3 {Develop and hold e4.} 5... Ng6
{The knight arrives. [%weight 6]} 6. d4
{The break, with everything ready for it. [%tags d4-break]} 6... exd4
{Black takes. [%weight 7]} 7. Nxd4 {Recapture, eyeing f5.} 7... Qf6
{Black develops with a hit on the knight. [%weight 6]} 8. O-O
{King first: the knight has enough defenders. [%tags king-safety] [%plan Play Be3 or Nde2 and Ng3, castle the rooks, and trade into the ending. The structure has not changed since move four.] [%line The Cozio, 3...Nge7]}
( 8. Nxc6? {The knight is not hanging: 8...bxc6 and White has given a piece for a pawn.} )
) 4. Bxc6
{Take anyway. The structure is what this course is about. [%tags doubled-pawns bishop-pair]}
( 4. O-O!? {The main Berlin, and an enormous amount of theory. Not this course.} ) (
4. d3!? {Solid and quiet, and it gives up the structure the whole repertoire is built on.}
) 4... dxc6 {Towards the centre, as always. [%weight 8]} 5. O-O
